   "source": [
    "**Observations**\n",
    "\n",
    "- **56 of the 136 sensor pairs have |r| > 0.8.** The four temperature sensors correlate at 0.998–1.0 with each other, and PS5, PS6 and FS2 move with temperature (r ≈ 0.99). PS1 and PS2 correlate at 0.995.\n",
    "- PS4 is almost uncorrelated with PS1 and PS2 (|r| < 0.05), so it carries independent information.\n",
    "- **Implication for clustering:** temperature-related information is repeated across many features, so in Euclidean distance it effectively gets extra weight. The clustering will be pulled towards the cooler/temperature state. The features are kept as they are to keep preprocessing simple; this weighting effect is noted as a limitation.\n",
    "- **Implication for PCA:** strong redundancy means a few components should capture most of the variance, which makes the 2D plots in Section 9 informative."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a1188c04",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Preprocessing\n",
    "Cleaning checks, then scaling.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Constant features are dropped** — a feature with zero variance cannot help separate cycles.\n",
    "- **No train/test split.** Clustering has no target to predict, so there is nothing to hold out; all 2,205 cycles are used. Leakage is not a concern because no labels are used in fitting.\n",
    "- **Scaling is required.** K-Means and Ward linkage use Euclidean distance, and features are in very different units (bar, W, °C), so unscaled features with large values would dominate. `StandardScaler` gives every feature mean 0 and standard deviation 1."
   ]
  },
