   "source": [
    "## 12. Conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "| | K-Means (k = 4) | Agglomerative (ward, k = 4) |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Silhouette ↑ | **0.573** | 0.572 |\n",
    "| Davies-Bouldin ↓ | **0.702** | 0.704 |\n",
    "| Calinski-Harabasz ↑ | **2488** | 2468 |\n",
    "| Usable for new cycles | Yes (`predict`) | No |\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Choices:** k = 4 from the elbow curve, confirmed by the dendrogram; ward linkage, the only linkage that produced meaningful clusters.\n",
    "- **Result:** both algorithms find the same four groups (ARI between them ≈ 0.98). K-Means is marginally better on all three metrics and is the one deployed in the app.\n",
    "- **Meaning:** without seeing any labels, the clusters map onto real health states. Three follow the cooler's condition (healthy, degraded, close to failure), and one isolates a **critical multi-fault state**: a failing cooler combined with pump leakage and low accumulator pressure. This complements the classification track: classification flags *whether* a machine fails, while clustering shows *what state* it is in.\n",
    "- **Limitations:**\n",
    "  - Mean, std, min and max lose the timing of switching transients, so valve degradation is not detected.\n",
    "  - Temperature information is repeated across many correlated features, so it dominates the distance measure; reducing redundancy (e.g. clustering on principal components) could expose other components.\n",
    "  - The 2D PCA view keeps 77% of the variance; t-SNE shows sub-groups that k = 4 merges.\n",
    "- **Possible next steps:** frequency- or transient-based features for the valve, clustering within each cooler state, or a larger k guided by the t-SNE sub-groups."
   ]
  }
